But we can extract from mapped_rxn strings? For template 1 mapped_rxn: '(CC(C)(C)[Si](C)(C)[O:17][C@H:16]1[CH2:15][C@H:6]... )>>(... )' The product side is after >>. That product SMILES is the deprotected? The product side includes [CH3:1]/[C:2](=[CH:3]/[C@H:4]1[O:5][C@@H:6]... etc. That product likely is the target? Actually forward reaction: protected -> deprotected? The forward would break O-Si, forming alcohol. So product is alcohol (no Si). So product SMILES is the deprotected molecule. So target (for retrosynthesis) is the protected one (reactant side). So we need the reactant side SMILES from mapped_rxn. That's the SMILES with Si. That is the target. We could extract but it's long. Could use the first precursor SMILES as target? It matches.

Thus we can set root smiles as "C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](C/C=C/C=C/C(=O)O[Si](C)(C)C(C)(C)C)C[C@H](O)[C@H]1C)CO" (first from apply_template-1). Then include reaction nodes for each template that applies to this molecule? But template 2 also applied to same molecule? The apply_template-2 gave precursors list with two SMILES: one similar to above but without Si? Actually first is same as above but without Si? It is "C/C(=C/[C@H]1O[C@@H](C/C=C/C=O)C[C@H](O)[C@H]1C)CO" (no Si). So template 2 applied to target (protected) to give aldehyde and phosphonate? So we can have another reaction node under root with template_id 74197 leading to two precursors.

Similarly template 3 applied to target? The precursor list is same as first of template 2 (no Si). So maybe sequential.

Template 4 also applied to target giving two precursors: same deprotected plus some ester.

